<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sudan</h1><p class="st-meta">1956 CE – present · capital Khartoum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Northeast African state independent since 1956, ruled mostly by military governments and divided by civil wars, the latest begun in 2023.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/anglo-egyptian-sudan">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a>, run as a British possession under a condominium agreed in 1899, was declared independent on 1 January 1956. A parliament had been elected, and Ismail al-Azhari became the first prime minister and raised the new flag. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s revolution of 1952 had set the move to independence in motion.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Colonel Gaafar Nimeiry took power in a coup on 25 May 1969 and abolished parliament and political parties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> Agreement of 1972 paused the north-south civil war, which resumed in the Second Sudanese Civil War and ended with the Comprehensive Peace Agreement of 2005. Omar al-Bashir seized power on 30 June 1989 and ruled until 2019, a period that included the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> from 2003, which has been described as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> separated in 2011 after a referendum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the state religion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law applied from 1983 until 2020.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Protests that began in December 2018 led to Bashir's removal on 11 April 2019 and a joint military and civilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> Council. General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan led a coup on 25 October 2021. Since 15 April 2023 the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces have fought a civil war in which both sides are accused of war crimes, with millions displaced.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sudan.jpg" alt="Sudan" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sudan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1956 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khartoum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ismail al-Azhari, Gaafar Nimeiry, Omar al-Bashir, Abdalla Hamdok, Abdel Fattah al-Burhan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (Sufi and Salafi), Christian minorities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/south-sudan">South Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of the Sudan, Republic of Sudan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1991&amp;focus=sudan" aria-label="Sudan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Sudan lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5208 6216L5208 6216M5208 6216L5135 7081"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5208 6216h0M5135 7081h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 6216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5208 6216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khartoum. <a href="/atlas?year=1991&amp;focus=sudan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
